Hkz,

I successfully got my old VAX 11/730 (R80/RL02 with TU80 config) running 
OpenVMS Hobbyist 7.3 last winter in time for its 25th anniversary.  The poor 
thing has barely enough horsepower & memory to get out of its own way!  I 
too suffered through bad power supply components and a TU58 capstan rot.  I 
am really surprised the R80 still works.  The 730's electonics & mechanicals 
are hobbyist repairable, although I have the field service prints and a 
decent set of spare boards from another 730 system -- which helps!  If 
anyone is interested in acquiring this restored antique, let me know... I 
need to recover space in my basement.

I fixed the TU58 capstans by replacing the degraded rubber with some tygon 
tubing as mentioned here:
http://www.vintage-computer.com/vcforum/showthread.php?t=4
This fix is acceptable, but I may still try to find some actual TU58 
replacement capstans (some DEC parts companies I contacted wanted $50 
each!).

Regarding the missing memory board, if you have other M8750 boards in the 
system, move them up to fill slot 6; you need at least one 1 MB memory board 
there to run the system.  I have some spare M8750 memory boards available if 
you want to contact me privately.

Regarding your CPU diagnostics & emulated TU58, are you sure the console is 
loading ALL the CPU microcode properly on power-on?  The 730/725 bit slice 
VAX processor will be brain damaged with any bad or missing microcode.

On a 730/725 with real TU58s, you should see the system spin up the internal 
& external drives trying find CONSLE.EXE on the tapes when powered on. 
After that it should execute the POWER.CMD file on the tape which loads up 
CONSLE.CPU, MMIE.CPU, POWER.CPU and then looks to see what hardware options 
are installed in the system (FPA, R80/RL02 IDC, etc).  A returned bit vector 
then determines which CODExx.CMD runs and loads more hardware dependent 
microcode (FP.CPU, FPSP.CPU, BITFLD.CPU, CM.CPU, BASIC.CPU, IRDFPA.CPU, 
QUEUE.CPU and/or IDC.CPU).  After that the default boot (DEFBOO.CMD) and 
various other device/diagnostic boot option command files are available.

I had 3 copies of the V58 microcode console tapes (master & 2 backups, each 
with different bad tape sections) and needed to manually load the microcode 
files (swapping tapes back & forth) to get everything loaded properly.  Once 
I got the 730 booted to VMS, I could finally work on piecing together a 
new/good master console tape again with EXCHANGE.  I have a VMS EXCHANGE 
image copy of the V58 microcode if you need it.

Regards,
Duane D. Krahn
Wauwatosa, WI  USA
ddkrahn@wi.rr.com



